---
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

interface Props {
  h1: string;
}

const { h1 } = Astro.props;
---
  <h1>{h1}</h1>

<h2 id="resumen">Summary <i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> </h2>

<p>
  Website where you can play different casino games and bet
</p>

<p>
  <strong>GitHub repository <i class="fab fa-github"></i>: </strong>
  <a href="https://github.com/Franco2900/Practica-2---Casino" target="_blank">Practice 2</a>
</p>

<h2 id="tecnologias">Technologies used <i class="bi bi-laptop"></i> </h2>

<div class="row">

  <div class="col-4">
    Backend: <br>
    NodeJS <i class="fab fa-node-js"></i> with the Express framework 
  </div>

  <div class="col-4">
    Database: <br>
    MongoDB <img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/simple-icons@v5/icons/mongodb.svg" alt="MongoDB logo" width="24" height="24">
  </div>
  
  <div class="col-4">
    Frontend: <br>
    Bootstrap <i class="fab fa-bootstrap"></i> and EJS
  </div>
  
</div>
<br>

<h2 id="descripcion">Full project description <i class="fas fa-align-left"></i> </h2>

<p>
  A website where users can spend their chips to play different casino games. Originally
  it was going to have several more games, but with only 2 games it works well enough to show the project running correctly. 
  You can play the slot machine and Blackjack.
  <br />

  &emsp;&emsp;The games are another of my university assignments. In the first years we are asked to build 
  different games in Python, to mix a programming language we had never seen until then with
  something fun. The games my group made were casino games, so I decided to redo them but in
  JavaScript and making them feel more like a real casino by adding a chip system with which the
  player can choose how many chips to bet.
  <br />

  &emsp;&emsp;This time I took the opportunity to study how to better organize the structure of the projects, because
  in the previous one, route handling, server logic and the database CRUD were all
  controlled in the same file. In a small project this may not cause any problem, but in larger
  projects it makes development and maintenance considerably more complex.
  <br />

  &emsp;&emsp;I also took the chance to review MongoDB, since only one course is taught on that 
  database.
  <br />

</p>

